# Fingerprints gitleaks may skip, each with the reason it is not a secret.
#
# `gitleaks git` scans the ENTIRE history, not the commits being pushed, so a finding in
# a commit that is already on the remote blocks every future push until it is resolved
# here or the history is rewritten. Rewriting main to remove a published public key would
# be a far bigger action than the finding warrants.
#
# 31c5fc8 — the p= value of resend._domainkey.studioface.app, quoted in HANDOFF.md while
# writing up the DMARC work. A DKIM public key is published in DNS for every receiving
# mail server on the internet to read; that is the entire point of it. It is high-entropy
# base64, which is why the generic-api-key rule fires, and it is not a secret in any
# sense. The bytes have since been removed from both HANDOFF.md and docs/verified.md,
# which now say an RSA public key is present and leave it at that — so this entry covers
# one historical commit and nothing going forward.
#
# Anything added here needs a line saying why. An allowlist without reasons is an
# allowlist nobody can audit, and this file is the one place a real secret could be
# waved through.
